. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H3 * (Please adapt according to your needs)
. 
. 
.         * Please add a verbal description of your model here
. 
. * We investigate whether having a daughter is associated with more gender-equ
> al behaviors or outcomes in the household. The dependent variable is the with
> in couple gender pay gap in percent, relative to the male partner's wage. We 
> only include couples in which both partners are working. We restrict the samp
> le to participants for whom we can link both partners via pgpartnr and confir
> m they share the same household ID (hid). We construct a couple dataset by sa
> ving female records (suffix_f) and male records (suffix_m), and then merging 
> these based on partner linkage (pgpartnr) and household id (hid). We only kee
> p those for which we have information from both partners living in one househ
> old. All time-invariant and time-varying control variables are included for b
> oth partners, identified by the suffixes _f (female) and _m (male). For some 
> controls — specifically east, numchild, and childu16, we only use the female 
> partner's value (_f) as explained in the PAP. 
. 
. * Based on the variables listed in our PAP, we choose the gender pay gap as o
> ur main outcome variable. The results with the alternative outcomes using the
>  gender workhours gap, the gender childcare gap and the gender housework gap,
>  measured by time use data, are available upon request. 
. 
. * Interpretation:
. * - Coefficient on d_daughter captures whether having at least one daughter i
> s associated with more gender-equal behaviors or outcomes in the household.
. * - Controls include age, education, migration background, marital status of 
> both partners and the number of children, number of children under 16 and reg
> ion (East) based on the female partner's value and survey year fixed effects.
. 
. * - Standard errors are clustered at the individual level.
. 
. * Disclaimer:
. * In our PAP, we proposed three alternative model specifications (1)w/o contr
> ols, 2)with core controls and 3)with additional controls) to test the hypothe
> sis. However, as per the current request to report only a single regression a
> nd associated p-value, we have selected the specification we consider most ap
> propriate and transparent for capturing the effect. In our PAP this specifica
> tion is listed as sepcification 2, i.e. the model with core controls. As expl
> ained on page 2, sentence one in our PAP, it includes key demographic charact
> eristics of the respondent, including age, gender, education, migration backg
> round, East/West Germany indicator, employment variables (where this is not t
> he outcome), and marital status. Note we forgot to list the marital status an
> d the education variable in our table but included it here now, as we did men
> tion it in our text. This model is reported and submitted in the present do-f
> ile. The results from the alternative specifications mentioned in the PAP are
>  available upon request. 
. 
. keep if lfs_f==1 & lfs_f_m==1
